Apple has discontinued the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/mac-pro/">Mac Pro</a> and has removed the machine from its website, reports <em><a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/03/26/apple-discontinues-the-mac-pro/">9to5Mac</a></em>. Apple said it does not plan to design a new version of the &zwnj;Mac Pro&zwnj;, and no new model will be coming in the future.<br/>
<br/>
<img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-880543" src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2023/01/Mac-Pro-Feature-Teal.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" /><br/>
The &zwnj;Mac Pro&zwnj; was last updated in 2023, which was when Apple added an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/m2/">M2</a> Ultra Apple silicon chip, but the chassis has not been refreshed since 2019. Apple redesigned the &zwnj;Mac Pro&zwnj; to be more modular in 2019 after failing with its <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2024/12/19/trashcan-mac-pro-11-years-ago/">"innovative" trashcan Mac Pro</a>, but the machine has never been mainstream due to its &#36;6,999 starting price.<br/>
<br/>
Apple has largely replaced the &zwnj;Mac Pro&zwnj; with the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/mac-studio/">Mac Studio</a>, a device that is smaller and uses newer Apple silicon chips. The &zwnj;Mac Studio&zwnj; is now Apple's high-end desktop machine designed for professional use.<br/>
<br/>
The current &zwnj;Mac Studio&zwnj; features an M3 Ultra chip, though it is expected to get an M5 Ultra refresh later this year. Apple's desktop lineup also includes the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/mac-mini/">Mac mini</a> and the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/imac/">iMac</a>.<br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;Mac Pro&zwnj;'s downfall started in 2013 when Apple introduced a radical cylindrical design that turned out to be a major mistake. The &zwnj;Mac Pro&zwnj;'s components were mounted around a central thermal dissipation core and cooled with a single fan that pulled air from under the case, through the core, and out of the top of the machine. It was quiet, but not efficient.<br/>
<br/>
When Apple announced the 2013 &zwnj;Mac Pro&zwnj;, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/phil-schiller/">Phil Schiller</a> infamously said "Can't innovate anymore, my ass," in response to critics who complained about the &zwnj;Mac Pro&zwnj;'s lack of updates and Apple's failure to create products for pro users.<br/>
<br/>
Unfortunately, the 2013 &zwnj;Mac Pro&zwnj;'s design did not include PCIe expansion slots for graphics cards and other hardware, with expansion handled through Thunderbolt 2 ports. The design also did not account for future updates in GPU technology, leaving Apple unable to add larger graphics cards and other components to the device.<br/>
<br/>
Apple ended up <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2017/04/04/apple-stresses-commitment-to-pro-users/">apologizing to its pro user base</a> and said the 2013 design was thermally constrained in a way that made upgrades impossible. It took Apple until 2019 to unveil the current &zwnj;Mac Pro&zwnj;, which adopted a more standard tower form factor with eight PCIe slots.<br/>
<br/>
